---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { rankedCount, sources, states } from "../lib/data";
import { MIN_DISTRICTS } from "../lib/fairness";
import { sourceNote } from "../lib/format";

const boundarySources = sources.filter((s) => s.role === "boundaries");
const voteSources = sources.filter((s) => s.role === "votes");
// The 50 state redistricting files share one credit, linked to the Census folder that holds them.
const populationSources = sources.filter((s) => s.role === "population");
const populationFolder = populationSources[0]?.url.replace(/[^/]+\/[^/]+$/, "") ?? "";
const populationDownloaded = populationSources.map((s) => s.downloaded).sort().at(-1) ?? "";
const calibrationCount = sources.filter((s) => s.role === "calibration").length;
const redrawn = states.filter((s) => s.changed_since_2024).map((s) => s.name);
---

<Base title="How scores work | Gerrymandering Map" path="/methodology" description="How this site measures the shape of every congressional district, and what the scores can and can't tell you.">
  <main>
    <h1>How scores work</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      This site measures how compact each congressional district's shape is. A compact district
      is roughly round or square. An irregular one snakes, sprawls or has pieces cut out of it.
      Each state page also scores the state's map for partisan fairness, explained
      {" "}<a href="#fairness">below</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>The three measures</h2>
    <p>Each runs from 0 to 1, where 1 is as compact as a shape can be.</p>
    <dl>
      <dt>Polsby-Popper</dt>
      <dd>
        Compares a district's area with its perimeter. A circle scores 1. Jagged edges and long
        thin arms add perimeter without adding area, so they pull the score down.
      </dd>
      <dt>Reock</dt>
      <dd>
        Compares a district's area with the smallest circle that fits around it. A stretched-out
        district fills little of its circle and scores low.
      </dd>
      <dt>Convex hull</dt>
      <dd>
        Compares a district's area with a rubber band stretched around it (its "convex hull"). A
        district with bites taken out of it, or tentacles reaching out, scores low.
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <h2 id="state-edge">Measuring against land</h2>
    <p>
      Reock and convex hull count only the part of the circle or rubber band that falls on the
      district's own state. Water between islands or past a coastline is space no district could
      use, so it doesn't count against a district. Land a district leaves out still does.
      Without this, island districts such as Hawaii's 2nd or the Florida Keys would rank among
      the least compact for their geography alone.
    </p>
    <p>
      Polsby-Popper has no such adjustment, so a ragged coastline still lowers it. Each district
      is measured in an equal-area map projection centered on the district, so a district in
      Alaska is measured the same way as one in Maine.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each district page also says how much of the district's border is the state's own edge: a
      coastline, a national border or a state line. Mapmakers don't choose those lines, and a
      ragged one, like a coastline or a river, lowers Polsby-Popper. A stretch of border counts
      as the state's edge if it lies within 100 meters of the state's outline, which allows for
      the small shifts from simplifying the newer state maps (see "Which map" below).
    </p>

    <h2>From three measures to one score</h2>
    <p>
      For each measure, a district gets a percentile: where it falls among the {rankedCount}
      districts that have lines to draw. The six states with a single district covering the whole
      state are measured but not ranked. The <strong>irregularity score</strong> is 100 minus the
      average of the three percentiles, so 0 is the most compact district and scores near 100 are
      the least compact. Percentiles put the three measures on one scale, since their raw values
      sit in very different ranges.
    </p>
    <p>
      "Less compact than X% of districts" on each district's page comes straight from its rank
      by irregularity score.
    </p>

    <h2>What a score can't tell you</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Intent.</strong> An irregular shape is not proof of a gerrymander, and a compact
        one is not proof against it. A map of neat squares can still be drawn to favor one party.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Good reasons for odd shapes.</strong> Districts follow rivers, coastlines, county
        and city lines, and communities that belong together. The Voting Rights Act can require
        districts that keep a minority community together, and those can be irregular on purpose.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Comparisons with other sites.</strong> Measured perimeter depends on how detailed
        the map is, so these scores should only be compared with each other.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="population">Who lives in each district</h2>
    <p>
      District pages note when most of a district's voting-age residents (18 and over) belong to
      minority groups: either one group is over half, or minority groups together are. The
      Voting Rights Act can require districts like these, so a minority community can elect
      candidates it prefers, and they can be irregular on purpose. The note doesn't say a
      district was drawn for that reason, because the site can't know that.
    </p>
    <p>
      The counts come from the 2020 Census redistricting data, the same data the maps were drawn
      with. Each census block is placed in the district that contains the point the Census marks
      inside it, and the blocks are added up. Hispanic or Latino residents of any race are one group. The other
      groups (Black, Asian, American Indian or Alaska Native, and Native Hawaiian or Pacific
      Islander) count people who aren't Hispanic and who gave that race alone or in combination
      with another, as Voting Rights Act analyses do, so someone who is Black and white counts as
      Black. Minority residents are everyone except non-Hispanic white residents.
    </p>
    <p>
      As a check, each district's 2020 population should equal its state's average, since every
      map in use was drawn to equal populations. Most are within 0.1%, typically a few dozen
      people. Districts in nine states are off by more, up to 2.3%, because those states adjust
      the Census counts before drawing maps: eight of them count people in prison at their home
      addresses rather than at the prison, and Hawaii leaves out military members and students
      who live elsewhere.
    </p>

    <h2>Which map</h2>
    <p>
      Scores are for the districts used in the November 3, 2026 election. Most come from the U.S.
      Census Bureau's map of the 2024 districts. {redrawn.length} states used new lines in 2026
      ({redrawn.join(", ")}), and for those the site uses each state's official 2026 map.
      Missouri and Virginia passed new maps that were not used in 2026, so they keep their earlier
      lines.
    </p>
    <p>
      The state files are much more detailed than the Census map, and more detail means a longer
      measured perimeter. So the new maps are simplified to the Census map's level of detail
      first. The settings were calibrated against the Census Bureau's own detailed and simplified
      versions of the same districts for 12 states, using {calibrationCount} extra Census files.
    </p>

    <h2 id="fairness">Partisan fairness</h2>
    <p>
      Shape is one way to look at a map. Another is how it turns votes into seats: whether one
      party's voters are packed into a few districts it wins by huge margins, or spread thin across
      districts it narrowly loses. Each state page scores this with three common measures. All three
      use the same sign: <strong>positive favors Republicans, negative favors Democrats</strong>, and
      0 favors neither. State pages show the size of the number and the party it favors in words.
    </p>
    <dl>
      <dt>Efficiency gap</dt>
      <dd>
        A vote is "wasted" if it went to a losing candidate, or to a winner beyond the half of the
        district's votes needed to win. The efficiency gap is Democratic wasted votes minus Republican
        wasted votes, as a share of all votes. A gap of 10% means one party wastes more votes than the
        other by 10% of all the votes cast.
      </dd>
      <dt>Mean-median difference</dt>
      <dd>
        The Democratic share of the vote averaged across the state's districts, minus the Democratic
        share in its middle (median) district, in percentage points. If the middle district is less
        Democratic than the average, Democratic voters are concentrated in a few districts, which
        favors Republicans. The reverse favors Democrats.
      </dd>
      <dt>Declination</dt>
      <dd>
        Lines up the districts from most Republican to most Democratic and compares how lopsided each
        party's wins are, as an angle scaled from -1 to 1 (Warrington, 2018). It needs districts won by
        both parties, so it can't be measured where one party carried every district.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <h3>Where the votes come from</h3>
    <p>
      The measures use the 2024 presidential vote (Kamala Harris and Donald Trump only, so third
      parties don't count) as a consistent measure of how each district leans. House races are less
      even: some candidates run unopposed, and incumbents draw extra votes. For states that used the
      same districts in 2024 and 2026, precinct results are added up within each district. Texas and
      North Carolina drew new districts for 2026 and published official 2024 results for them, so
      those are used directly. The other redrawn states published none, so their scores will use the
      2026 House results once they are final.
    </p>
    <h3>What fairness scores can't tell you</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Small states.</strong> With few districts, one seat changing hands swings every
        measure, so only states with {MIN_DISTRICTS} or more districts are scored.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>States one party sweeps.</strong> When one party carries every district, the
        efficiency gap is large however the lines are drawn, since every vote for the other party
        counts as wasted. Where a party's voters are spread evenly across a state, no map may give it
        a district.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Intent, or one election.</strong> These measures describe how a map performed with
        one set of votes. Where voters live (cities, for example) shapes the numbers as much as where
        the lines go, and the scores shift from one election to the next.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Data sources</h2>
    <h3>District maps</h3>
    <ul class="sources">
      {
        boundarySources.map((s) => (
          <li>
            <a href={s.url}>{s.publisher}</a>: {sourceNote(s.description, s.license, s.downloaded)}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <h3>Population</h3>
    <ul class="sources">
      <li>
        <a href={populationFolder}>U.S. Census Bureau</a>: 2020 Census Redistricting Data (PL 94-171),
        one file for each state. Public domain (U.S. Government work). Downloaded {populationDownloaded}.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Votes</h3>
    <ul class="sources">
      {
        voteSources.map((s) => (
          <li>
            <a href={s.url}>{s.publisher}</a>: {sourceNote(s.description, s.license, s.downloaded)}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p>
      Every step, from download to score, is open source and can be rerun:
      {" "}<a href="https://github.com/rpivo/gerrymandering-map">github.com/rpivo/gerrymandering-map</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The code is under the MIT License. The scores, ranks and shapes this site produces are under
      {" "}<a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">Creative Commons Attribution 4.0</a>:
      you can reuse them for any purpose if you credit Gerrymandering Map.
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  main {
    max-width: 42rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 1.5rem 1rem;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 5vw, 2.5rem);
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  h2 {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    font-size: 1.25rem;
  }

  h3 {
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  dt {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  dd {
    margin: 0.25rem 0 0;
  }

  li + li {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }

  .sources {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
</style>
